Swedish law has no rule against hitchhiking (''lifta''), so on ordinary roads it is simply legal; only the fast roads are closed to you. [https://www.riksdagen.se/sv/dokument-och-lagar/dokument/svensk-forfattningssamling/trafikforordning-19981276_sfs-1998-1276/ Chapter 9 of the ''trafikförordning'' (1998:1276)] — the whole of the law on ''motorväg'' and ''motortrafikled'', two sections long — admits only motor vehicles that are built for and may be driven at at least 40 km/h, which leaves pedestrians with nowhere lawful to stand, and it forbids stopping and parking outside the places marked for it. That reaches the hard shoulder and the slip road past the blue sign, so thumb on the on-ramp before it. Ask for lifts at a ''rastplats'' or a motorway petrol station, where drivers have already stopped and you can speak to them. On ''riksvägar'', ''länsvägar'' and everything smaller, standing on the verge with your thumb out breaks no rule.
